    meditation: `There is a file in your cabinet — the precise location does not matter; you know which one — that has been open for three years. The notice of appeal was filed in a different season of your life. You wrote the opening brief in a coffee shop that has since closed. Oral argument was held twenty months ago. You sent the client a holiday card last December that did not mention the case because there was nothing new to mention. Every six weeks or so you log into the docket and see the same entry: SUBMITTED. The court is thinking. The court has been thinking for a long time.

This is the slow case. Every lawyer who practices long enough has one. The appellate matter where the panel cannot agree. The administrative claim that has sat with the agency since before the last election. The estate that cannot close because one heir cannot be found. The disability remand from the district court that came back to the ALJ who has now retired. The slow case is not a failure of your practice. It is a feature of the system itself, and it teaches a virtue your law school did not have a class for.

The virtue is patience, and the slow case is its school. James writes to a church under pressure and gives them the image of the farmer. Behold, the husbandman waiteth for the precious fruit of the earth: patiently bearing till he receive the early and latter rain. The farmer's patience is not passive. He is not a man who has given up on the harvest; he is a man who knows precisely what he is waiting for, in what season, and what he is bound to do in the meantime — clear the ditches, mend the fences, ready the barn. Patience, in the biblical idiom, is active waiting. It is the discipline of doing what is yours to do without yet seeing the thing you most want to see.

The slow case asks you for that discipline in a particular form. You cannot make the court rule. You cannot bribe the calendar. You cannot, however many billable hours you throw at it, advance the matter by one day past what the panel decides. What you can do is small and unglamorous: you can keep the client informed; you can refile your appearance when chambers rotates clerks; you can confirm that the address on file is still where the client lives; you can read, once a quarter, the latest cases from the circuit in the area of law your matter touches, in case there is a holding you will want to bring to the panel's attention by letter under the rule. The slow case is a long act of being faithful in small things while the large thing does not happen.

Notice what the slow case is doing to you, if you let it. It is unlearning the cadence the rest of your week trained you in. The rest of your week is fast. Email is fast. The bench is impatient. The intake call wants an answer today. The associate above you wants the draft by five. Your nervous system has been trained, over years of practice, to read urgency as the only key of operation. The slow case sits there, unmoved, and refuses to play in that key. It teaches your soul a different tempo. The teaching is not pleasant. It feels, often, like wasted time. It is not wasted time. It is the only place in your practice where you are being formed in the long-form virtue, and the long-form virtue is the one you will most need when something genuinely matters.

There is a temptation, of course, to drop the file. To stop checking. To let the case become invisible to you because it has become invisible to the court. The temptation is small and quiet and almost reasonable: nothing has happened in eight months; surely a month off the file will hurt no one. The temptation is to be resisted because the client has not been able to take a month off the case. The case is the largest thing in her life. She thinks about it on the bus to work; she dreams about it; she rehearses what she will say if the ruling comes down badly. Your discipline of remembering her case on a quarterly schedule, even when nothing has changed, is a small share in her bearing it. You are the husbandman of someone else's harvest. You wait for a fruit that is not for you.

The early Church Fathers, when they wrote on patience, never separated it from hope. Patientia in Latin and hypomone in Greek are not the virtues of the resigned. They are the virtues of those who expect something — who would not bother to endure if they did not expect — and who, expecting, do not collapse the time between now and the expected thing into despair. The slow case demands this of you. You expect a ruling; you do not know when; you do not know which way. You hold the expectation without letting it sour into either certainty or cynicism. That holding is patience.

There is also a particular kind of prayer the slow case teaches. The fast file gets prayed for by acclamation: Lord, the hearing is tomorrow, please. The slow case gets prayed for by repetition: the same case, on the same list, brought before God again on Wednesday because it was brought before God last Wednesday too. You will pray for that file fifty more times before the ruling comes down. The prayers will not sound impressive. They will sound like a man asking a long-suffering friend a question he has already asked. That is what they are. It is also, in its plain way, what intercession looks like — the same names, the same matters, the same God, week after week, until the ruling comes or the case ends or the parties die.

So go pull the file. Today, this week — pull it out, look at the notice of submission, log into the docket, write the client an email that says simply, no news yet, I have not forgotten you. Then put the file back. That small act is what patience looks like at the desk. It is also what the husbandman is doing when he walks the field in February and sees no sign of the harvest and goes home and sharpens his tools anyway.`,
