      text: "Patience is a virtue. For it belongs to virtue to safeguard the good of reason against any evil. Now sorrow is, of itself, an obstacle to the reason; wherefore the Philosopher says that sorrow drives out the joy of the spirit. Hence there is need of a virtue to safeguard the good of reason against sorrow, lest reason give way to sorrow: and this is the work of patience. . . . Patience does not have its principal act in enduring evils that come from other men, but in bearing those that come from God: for it is not so much patience to endure injuries from men as to bear with equanimity adversities sent by divine providence. Yet the higher cause of patience is the love of God, by which the heart is enlarged so as not to be straitened by sorrow.",
      workId: "aquinas-summa",
