    meditation: `Thomas places patience in the Secunda Secundae among the virtues annexed to fortitude. The placement is not accidental. The Angelic Doctor will not let you read patience as a kind of pleasant disposition, a temperamental gift granted to mild-mannered people and denied to the rest of us. Patience is a strength. It is annexed to courage because the same soul that needs courage to act under fear needs patience to hold steady under sorrow. Both are about not being deflected from the good of reason by something that pushes against it. Courage holds against the threat that something terrible may happen. Patience holds against the burden that something terrible has happened or is now happening and will not stop happening soon.

This is worth holding still over, because the modern instinct misreads patience badly. We think of patience as low-grade tolerance — the capacity to put up with a slow waiter or a child's question. Thomas would smile at the description and then redirect. Patience for him is what holds the rational soul in place when grief, frustration, and tedium would unseat it. It is the virtue that lets a man continue to think clearly, and act rightly, in conditions that would, without it, make him irrational.

A lawyer should recognize this immediately. Your worst professional decisions were not made when you were rested and unbothered. They were made when sorrow, in Thomas's broad sense, had begun to crowd out the good of reason. You were furious about a discovery violation and you fired off the email you should have slept on. You were exhausted from a long deposition and you missed the question you would have caught on a normal Tuesday. You were grieving the loss of a case and you took on the next one without doing the conflicts check carefully. Patience, in Thomas's account, is the virtue that would have kept reason in place in each of those hours. Its absence is the explanation for half your regrets.

In article four of the same question Thomas does something even harder. He says the principal act of patience is not enduring injuries from other men but bearing with equanimity the adversities sent by divine providence. The reasoning is theological but it cashes out very practically. To endure an injury from a colleague who treated you badly is real patience but limited patience; you can locate the cause and, in time, argue with it. To endure the adversities sent by providence — the slow case, the unexplained delay, the loss whose reason you will never know, the long erosion of a practice you cannot quite name — is harder. There is no one to argue with. There is only the long sentence of providence to bear with equanimity. That is the higher work of the virtue.

Thomas finishes the article with a striking sentence: the higher cause of patience is the love of God, by which the heart is enlarged so as not to be straitened by sorrow. The Latin underneath is non angustietur — that the heart be not made narrow. Sorrow narrows. Frustration narrows. The slow file narrows. The decade-long appeal narrows. The client who keeps you on the phone for forty minutes when you have a brief due narrows. Patience, in its highest cause, is the heart's enlargement against that narrowing — and the enlargement comes from love, from charity, not from technique. You do not become patient by trying harder to be patient. You become patient by being loved enough that the things that narrow you cannot quite succeed.

This is why Paul, in the verse from Romans, puts patience in a chain: tribulation worketh patience; and patience trial; and trial hope; and hope confoundeth not: because the charity of God is poured forth in our hearts. The chain begins in tribulation and ends in love. Patience is the link by which one becomes the other. Without patience, tribulation stays tribulation; it does not become trial in the testing sense, it becomes only injury. With patience, the tribulation does its slow work — and what it slowly works in you is the capacity to hold steady while God's love teaches you what it has to teach.

There is a question to ask yourself, then, about the trials of your current practice. The slow case, the difficult client, the partner whose ego you have to manage, the eight-day stretch of weekend document review that begins tomorrow. The question is not, How do I get through this? The question is, Will I let this work in me what it is trying to work — or will I refuse to be enlarged, and stay narrow? Refusal looks like irritability, contempt for those who do not understand, gallows humor that has lost the gallows part. Acceptance looks like — well, like the ordinary lawyer who, having borne much, can sit with the difficult client without being annoyed, and read the long file without skipping, and stand the slow case for the years it takes, and not despise any of it.

You will not produce this acceptance by gritting your teeth. The angle Thomas gives is different. Sit longer in the love that is being poured into your heart by the Holy Ghost. The patience will follow. The narrowing will slowly cease. The desk at which you sit will become — not always, not visibly, but in fact — a place where the good of reason is held, by grace, against everything that would press it out.

A note on the schoolroom of this. Thomas knew, as a Dominican, that the schoolroom of patience is the cell, the chapter, the long obedience of the Office. The lawyer's schoolroom is different but no less real. It is the morning hour with bad news on the screen. It is the Tuesday calendar that is twice as full as it should be. It is the call at eight from the probation officer about the client who is, again, in a jam. The schoolroom is where you actually live. Aquinas's question 136 was not written for you to admire it; it was written for you to find yourself in it. Find yourself, then. The virtue is being grown in you by exactly the conditions that you have been resenting.`,
