    meditation: `Job is the longest lawsuit in the Old Testament. The case lasts forty-two chapters. The plaintiff is a man who has lost ten children, his property, his health, and his standing in the community. The defendant, in the technical sense of the suit Job presses, is God himself. The friends are uninvited co-counsel who insist on settlement on terms Job knows to be false. The judge is the very party Job has sued. The only one who is honest about all of this is Job, who keeps insisting, against every social pressure, that he wants his day in court.

A lawyer should read Job differently than a layman. The book is written, in significant part, in juridical language. Job calls for an arbitrator (9:33). He asks God to file the bill of particulars against him (10:2). He demands a record be made of his words: O that my words were written! O that they were marked down in a book! With an iron pen and in a plate of lead, or else were graven with an instrument in flint stone (19:23-24). The patriarch is asking for what every lawyer asks for: discovery, transcript, an open hearing. He believes he is innocent of the charge his friends have brought, and he wants the matter heard.

This is patience of a very particular kind. Job is not the figure of the resigned sufferer that the casual reader takes him to be. The proverb in English, the patience of Job, has flattened him. He is, in fact, the man who refuses to be silent about the wrong that has been done to him, who refuses to accept the easy theology of his friends, who refuses to curse God and die. He is patient not because he is passive but because he is willing to wait — through long argument, through unanswered prayer, through the silence of God across many chapters — for the hearing he believes will come.

His patience is, in other words, the patience of a litigant in a slow court. Read his speeches and you will recognize the cadence. The mood swings between rage and resignation. The certainty that he is right alternates with the bewilderment that no one in the visible court agrees. He pleads his case to his friends; they will not hear him. He pleads it to himself; he cannot resolve it. He pleads it, finally, into the dark — to a Judge whom he cannot see and cannot summon, but whose existence he will not abandon. The whole book is a long act of bringing the case to a tribunal that does not, on its own timetable, convene.

There comes a moment, in the nineteenth chapter, when the patience cracks open into something stronger than itself. I know that my Redeemer liveth, and in the last day I shall rise out of the earth. The Latin Vulgate uses redemptor — the next-of-kin who appears at court to vindicate the relative who cannot vindicate himself. Job, after months or years of arguing his case without resolution, says he knows there is one who will appear. The Christian eye reads forward; the Jewish eye reads backward into the goel of Leviticus. Either way, the patience of Job is anchored in the conviction that there is an advocate who will, in the end, appear, and that the case will, in the end, be heard.

There is a lesson in this for the lawyer who is carrying the long matter. The temptation in the slow case is to stop expecting the hearing. The matter has been pending so long that you have come to read it as a permanent feature of your docket rather than as a case awaiting resolution. The settlement negotiations have dragged for three years; you have started to believe they will drag forever; you no longer prepare for the next session with the energy you brought to the first. The patience of Job is a corrective. The litigant who waits well is the one who, twenty months in, still believes the hearing is coming. He does not collapse the case into its delay. He keeps the file ready against the day the call comes.

There is also a quieter lesson, about the client. Many of your clients are Job. The mother whose deportation case has been pending for four years. The man who has been waiting for the disability hearing since the second knee surgery. The widow whose probate has been hung up on a missing heir for so long that the estate has been chewed away by the carrying costs. They have, like Job, lost much. They have, like Job, friends who tell them this must be because of something they did. They want a hearing; they cannot bring one themselves; they are sitting in a long silence. Your part is to be, in some small visible way, the redemptor — the one who keeps the case alive when the litigant has lost the strength to keep it alive on her own.

This is the deepest dignity of the slow case. The client cannot carry the matter through a five-year appeal. She does not have the legal vocabulary, the postage, the energy, the schedule, the sustained hope. You can. Your patience is, for her, a participation in the patience of a larger Redeemer; your willingness to keep the file open, to keep checking the docket, to keep returning her call after she has called every Wednesday for a year, is what is keeping her in the courtroom of the world rather than letting her drift, like Job, out into a wilderness where no one will hear her.

Gregory the Great, preaching the Moralia for years to his curial circle in Rome, would not let Job be merely a parable of suffering. He insisted Job was a juridical figure — a man preparing his plea while being wounded, setting in order his defense while the case was still being made against him, never abandoning the case even when the flesh failed. The patience Gregory praised was the patience that does not abandon the case. The lawyer's patience, in its small register, is the same.

So when you come home tonight and the client who has called you nine times this month calls a tenth time, and you can hear in her voice the slow erosion that her case has done to her — answer it. Do not let the case lapse on your side because it has lapsed on the court's side. Wait with her. The Redeemer liveth. The hearing will, in some form, come. Until then, your patience is what is holding her place in the matter.`,
