    meditation: `Augustine wrote the little treatise De Patientia toward the end of his life, when the Donatist controversy was still bleeding and the empire was beginning to crack. He had reason to know the subject. The book is brief, two hours of careful reading; it is one of the underread treasures of the Western tradition on the virtue. It does one thing supremely well: it refuses to let patience be a generic toughness.

There is a worldly patience, Augustine concedes. The miser is patient with the indignity of being thought stingy, because he loves his money more than he loves a good reputation; he endures the slight to keep the gold. The ambitious man is patient with humiliation from his patron, because he loves the promotion more than he loves his pride; he swallows the insult to keep the line of preferment open. The criminal is patient with the long planning of the crime, because he loves the prize more than he fears the labor. All of these endure things. None of them, Augustine says, has the virtue of patience. They have only a substitute that the love of the wrong thing produces.

This is bracing. Apply it. The associate who endures eighty-hour weeks not for the love of the work but for the love of the partnership track has a counterfeit patience. The litigator who endures the years of a difficult matter not because he loves the client but because he loves the eventual fee has a counterfeit patience. You, sitting at your desk on a Saturday morning, are not necessarily exercising the virtue simply because you are enduring the morning. The endurance has to be for the love of something true, or it is not the virtue Augustine is naming. It is, instead, a kind of laborious idolatry.

The lawyer's profession is full of counterfeit patiences. There is the patience that is really the calculation that the discomfort will pay off later. There is the patience that is really pride: you do not lose your temper because you do not want the others in the room to see you lose it, not because you have transcended the temper. There is the patience that is really cynicism: you bear opposing counsel's insults because you have come to believe nothing matters enough to push back. Augustine cuts through all of them. None is the virtue. The virtue requires the love of God as its root.

What does that look like in the daily mechanics of a practice? It looks, perhaps, like this. You are reading a brief from opposing counsel on a Friday afternoon. The brief is dishonest in places, sloppy in others, and contains a tone that you would not have used on the worst client you ever had. The bad patience would be to roll your eyes and draft a snide response. The worldly patience would be to swallow the response because you do not want to look intemperate in front of the court. The Augustinian patience is something else: you read the brief carefully, you set aside the wounded vanity, you respond to the substance with substance, you ignore the tone, and you do all this because you remember that you are not finally accountable to opposing counsel or to your own ego but to a God who has been patient with worse from you. The endurance is rooted somewhere outside the transaction. That is what makes it virtue.

Augustine grounds this in his own most famous experience. He had, by the time he wrote De Patientia, lived through his own long impatience — the years of intellectual restlessness, the years of resisting his mother's prayers, the years of half-believing the Manichees, the long delay in his own coming to baptism. The pearls of his Confessions are partly the testimony of a man who came to understand, late, how patient God had been with him. The treatise on patience is in some sense the work of a man saying: this is what was done for me, and this is what I am now bound to do for others.

There is a particular grace here for the lawyer who is impatient with himself. You will read this paragraph and think of the things you have been impatient about — the client whose call you cut short last week, the colleague whose question you snapped at, the prayer you started and abandoned. Augustine would not have you wallow in any of it. He would say only that the patience God has had with you is a deeper patience than any you are likely to extend, and that the model and source of your patience with others is not your willpower but his patience with you. The way to grow more patient with the world is to sit, often, with how patient God has been with your own slow conversion.

There is a sentence from the verse Luke gives today that the Greek Fathers loved: en te hypomone hymon, in your patience you shall possess your souls. Augustine cited it more than once. The Latin Vulgate reads in patientia vestra possidebitis animas vestras. The promise is strange: not that you will possess your goods, or your reputation, or your career, or your influence — but that you will possess your soul. Patience is the means by which the soul comes finally to belong to itself again. Without it, you do not have your soul. You have only the day's reactions to the day's grievances, scattered and unowned.

This is, in its old-fashioned way, a profoundly practical promise. The lawyer who has cultivated patience has himself in a way the impatient lawyer does not. He can sit through the difficult intake without dissolving. He can take the bad ruling without losing the rest of the afternoon. He can wait through the slow case without becoming bitter at the courts. He owns himself. He has not been farmed out, piece by piece, to every grievance and disappointment that came along.

So this evening, read three pages of De Patientia if you can find a copy. (Newman's edition, the old Library of the Fathers, is in the public domain; so is Schaff's.) If you cannot find it, sit instead with the verse: in your patience you shall possess your souls. Ask whether your patience this week was rooted in something true, or in something counterfeit. The Lord who was patient with Augustine for thirty years will be patient with you about the answer.`,
