    meditation: `It is Wednesday. The client calls. She is the one whose case you have been working since November. She asks you the question she asked you Monday. She asks it in the same words. The answer has not changed in two days. She does not remember that she asked. You can hear, in the slight tremor of her voice, that the case has been the only thing she has thought about for six months, and that the anxieties have begun to cycle. She does not know she is repeating. She is reaching, again, for the only piece of solid ground she has access to, which is your voice on the phone telling her what is going to happen next.

You have a brief due Friday. You have eleven other matters open. The intake from the morning is still on your desk waiting to be filed. You have, on the most generous calculation of your remaining hours this week, about three hours of margin. The call will take fifteen minutes. You know exactly which fifteen.

This is the small theater of patience. Not the heroic kind. Not the courtroom kind. The patience that lives at the corner of the desk where the phone rings and the calendar tightens. The patience that has to choose, in the half-second between recognizing the number and accepting the call, what kind of lawyer you are about to be in the next quarter hour.

Chrysostom, preaching the book of Acts in Antioch and later Constantinople, gave a homily on Paul's care for those who could not, by reason of suffering or simplicity, hear an instruction once and retain it. He pointed to Christ. Christ did not say, when the disciples asked him a second time about the parable of the sower, Have I not told you already? He answered the question. He did not register the inefficiency. He treated the second asking as if it were the first. The discipleship Chrysostom is teaching is, in part, a discipleship of forbearance with repetition — and the model is the patience of the Lord with disciples who, on the page of the Gospel, are slow in exactly the way our clients are.

A lawyer formed in this discipleship will say something simple at the start of the call: I am glad you called, Mrs. M. The greeting alone does work. It tells the client that the call is not an interruption — though it is — but a moment that has been included in your understanding of the day. You let her ask the question. You answer it. You answer it in the same words you used Monday because the same words on Wednesday are not wasted; they are a second laying down of the same paving stone in a path she is trying to walk. You may add a sentence she has not yet heard — a small piece of new news, even an obvious one: I checked the docket again this morning; nothing yet — because she needs the renewed sign of presence as much as she needs the information.

What you do not do is communicate, by tone or pacing, that the call is taking from you something you wanted for yourself. You may be communicating that to yourself, internally, and the discipline is to refuse to leak it. The discipline matters because the client will hear it if it is there. She will hang up faster than she should. She will not call next Wednesday with the question that actually matters. You will have saved fifteen minutes today and bought yourself a problem in eight weeks.

There is a deeper anthropology under this. The client who repeats herself is not, in fact, exercising your patience in some incidental way; she is exercising it as the very point of your office. The lawyer's vocation is not to serve the efficient client who comes prepared with bullet points and exits in nine minutes. The efficient client could do most of the work himself. The vocation is, in significant part, to be the person who can be in the same conversation with the same client for the seventh time and not have lost interest in her. The repetition is not a corruption of the work. It is the work in its truest form, because it is the work that no machine, no website, no flowchart can do — sit with a frightened person, answer the same question warmly, again, and not despise her for needing to ask.

This is also where your soul gets formed in a way you will not notice. The lawyer who handles the repetitive client well, over years, becomes — slowly, without trying — a different kind of person. He becomes a person who does not need others to be brief in order to love them. He becomes a person who can sit at the bedside of an elderly parent without checking his watch. He becomes a person his children can talk to about the same fear at age eight and age fourteen and age twenty-three without the conversation getting old. The patience grown in the small theater of the Wednesday call furnishes a soul for the larger theaters of marriage, parenting, friendship, late-life caretaking. It is not wasted.

There is, finally, a kindness in the design. The repetitive client is, as it were, God's chosen vessel for forming you in patience by means of small and frequent applications rather than rare and dramatic ones. The dramatic patience — the one that endures the year-long appeal — is hard because it is long. The small patience — the one that endures fifteen minutes — is hard because it is constant. You will not have to face a year-long appeal more than once a decade. You will face the Wednesday call every week. The Lord knew what he was doing when he placed the formation in the place where you cannot escape it.

So when the phone rings this afternoon, before you answer it, take one breath. Hold it for a count of two. Let it out. Then pick up. The client on the other end is a person Christ is patient with right now, in this very moment, on the other side of the line, on the other end of every line in the world. Your patience for the next fifteen minutes is a small share in his. You are not being interrupted from your real work. You are doing your real work. Open the file as she begins to speak, and let her ask, again, the question she asked Monday.`,
    prayer: `Lord, who answered the disciples' second question as if it were their first, give me the patience of a workman who does not despise the small theater. Let me hear my client's repeated fear as a fresh sound. Form me, by these Wednesdays, into a person who can sit at any bedside without checking his watch. Amen.`,
