    meditation: `The week closes where, in some sense, it should have begun: with the patience you owe yourself. The lawyer who has read every preceding day this week — patience with the slow case, with Aquinas's careful definition, with Job, with Augustine's distinction, with the repetitive client, with opposing counsel — will arrive at this day and feel, perhaps, a low ache. He has not measured up. He sent the email last Tuesday. He was short with the client on Wednesday. He stopped checking the docket on the slow case three months ago. He has caught himself, this week, in the very impatience he has been reading about. The natural impulse is a small flare of self-disgust, followed by a resolution, followed by a slow forgetting of the resolution, followed by the next failure, followed by the next flare.

Francis de Sales, in a passage of the Devout Life that has comforted three centuries of strugglers, will not allow this cycle. Be patient with all the world; but first of all with thyself. The Genevan bishop knew, from a thousand letters with anxious souls, that the impatient lawyer is, very often, a lawyer who is most impatient with himself. The sharpness he turns outward is a small leak from the larger sharpness he turns inward. Heal the inward and the outward will begin to ease. Refuse to heal the inward, and no amount of resolutions about the outward will produce the change you want.

This is harder than it sounds because the legal mind is, by training, a critical mind. You were taught to spot the flaw in the argument, the gap in the brief, the inconsistency in the deposition. You have been paid for twenty years to find what is wrong. The skill is precious in its place and lethal when turned, unfiltered, on your own soul. You audit yourself the way you audit a closing memo, and you find errors, and the errors mount, and by Friday afternoon you have prosecuted yourself rather thoroughly without ever putting on the case for the defense.

Francis distinguishes carefully. There is a correction that comes from self-love, vexed because the fault hinders progress. It sounds spiritual, but it is really vanity dressed in piety: I would be further along by now if I were not still doing this. It produces, he says, only deeper sinking. And there is a correction that comes from the love of God, sorrowful for the offense, gentle with the self, businesslike about repair. It produces rising. The two corrections look identical from the outside; one is a hammer, the other a hand on the shoulder. Only the inside knows which it is.

The lawyer who has not yet learned this distinction lives, often, in a low chronic anxiety that he reads as professional vigor. He calls it being hard on himself. He thinks it is a virtue. It is, in fact, a quiet form of pride, because it assumes that the project of his becoming a better lawyer is finally his own to drive by means of self-flagellation. The truer posture is the one Paul names in Philippians: I do not count myself to have apprehended. One thing I do: forgetting the things that are behind, and stretching forth myself to those that are before. Paul names the past as past. He does not relitigate it. He turns toward what is ahead. He presses on. The grammar is forward-looking, not retrospective.

This is the patience you owe yourself: the patience of the apostle who has not arrived, who knows he has not, who does not collapse the not-yet into a verdict against himself, and who keeps walking. The decade between the lawyer you are and the lawyer you might one day be will not be closed by impatience. It will be closed by the small, repeated, unimpressive choice to begin again — after the bad email, after the missed call, after the slow case forgotten, after the impatient response to opposing counsel. The beginning again is the actual virtue. Everyone fails. Saints are the ones who began again more times than anyone counted.

There is also a particular wound the lawyer carries that needs naming here: the wound of not being wise yet. You can see, just over your shoulder, the lawyer you would like to be — temperate, patient, kind, a steady hand at the difficult intake, a calm voice in opposing counsel deposition, a sustained intercessor for the slow case. You are not that lawyer. You are some distance behind him. The gap between the seen and the real is what most professional discouragement is, finally, made of. The Devout Life would have you look at the gap and not despair of it. The gap is exactly where the formation is happening. If you had no gap, you would have no need of grace, and you would in fact be a worse lawyer than you are, because you would be unable to receive any further help.

Quarter two is opening — week fifteen will turn to memory; week sixteen to friendship; eventually the year will press into virtues you have not yet examined and vices you will recognize on first reading. Walk into the second quarter the way Francis would have you walk into next Monday. Be patient with all the world; but first of all with thyself. Allow yourself the discovery that you are not yet wise. Be neither surprised nor scandalized. The not-yet is the place of the actual work. The work is being done by someone whose patience with you is longer than the year of this book and longer than the years of your life. Receive it. Then return to the cabinet.

There is one practice to try tonight. Make a small examen, but make it gentle. Three minutes. Where was I patient today? Where was I not? Where did grace help me to be better than I would have been an hour earlier? Do not extend the list of failures past one or two. Do not interrogate yourself. Name them; offer them; ask for tomorrow what you did not have today. Then close the page. The lawyer who does this kind of evening, four nights a week for a year, will discover at the end of the year that something has shifted — not by his own labor primarily, but by the patient working of God in the soft hours between the day's failures and the next morning's mercies. The Father who has been patient with you for forty years is not running out of patience tonight. Sleep, and rise tomorrow, and try again.`,
